+| `active_record.run_after_transaction_callbacks_in_order_defined` | `true` | `false` | **New.** Rails 7.0 runs `after_commit` callbacks in the reverse order of their definition, and `Issue` relies on it ("add_auto_watcher needs to run before sending notifications"): with `true`, the creation mail is sent before the author is added as a watcher, so an author notified as a watcher only isn't (`IssueTest#test_create_should_add_watcher_before_sending_the_notification`). Plugins may rely on the order too. | `Issue` declares its callbacks in the order they must run, and plugins are checked. |
+| `active_record.default_column_serializer` | `nil` | `YAML` | **New.** A model declaring `serialize :attr` without a coder raises when it's loaded (with eager loading: Redmine doesn't boot). Redmine always passes `coder: YAML`, plugins often don't. Stored data stays YAML either way. | Plugins pass `coder:`. |
+| `active_job.enqueue_after_transaction_commit` | `:default` | `:never` | **New.** Keeps enqueuing jobs (mails) immediately, like Rails 7.0 and like Rails 8 again by default (where the setting is ignored): no behaviour change for one release only. | The next release (no-op on Rails 8). |
+| `yjit` | `true` | `false` | **New.** YJIT adds about 63MB per process (measured on this application in production mode after 480 requests: 219MB -> 282MB resident, for ~5% less time). Production runs without YJIT today and its memory headroom isn't known. | Ops have checked the memory of every server (`RUBY_YJIT_ENABLE=1` or `config.yjit = true` in `config/additional_environment.rb` to try it). |
 | `action_controller.forgery_protection_origin_check` | `true` | `false` | A POST whose `Origin` doesn't match the host Rails sees fails the CSRF check, and Redmine signs the user out. That depends on the reverse proxy forwarding `Host` and `X-Forwarded-Proto`, which couldn't be verified. | Ops have checked the proxy (a form POST through it works with the check on, eg. in staging). |
-| `action_controller.raise_on_open_redirects` | `true` | `false` | `redirect_to_referer_or` (used by `redirect_back_or_default ..., :referer => true`) redirects to the `Referer` header. Rails raises `UnsafeRedirectError` (500) when it contains non-ASCII characters (`TimelogControllerTest#test_destroy_should_redirect_to_referer`) or when its host isn't the one Rails sees (proxy). Plugins may redirect to other hosts (eg. SSO). | `redirect_to_referer_or` validates the referer like `validate_back_url`, the proxy forwards `Host`, and plugins pass `allow_other_host: true` where needed. |
+| `action_controller.raise_on_open_redirects` (Rails 8.1: `action_on_open_redirect`) | `true` (`:raise`) | `false` (`:log`) | `redirect_to_referer_or` (used by `redirect_back_or_default ..., :referer => true`) redirects to the `Referer` header. Rails raises `UnsafeRedirectError` (500) when it contains non-ASCII characters (`TimelogControllerTest#test_destroy_should_redirect_to_referer`) or when its host isn't the one Rails sees (proxy). Plugins may redirect to other hosts (eg. SSO). | `redirect_to_referer_or` validates the referer like `validate_back_url`, the proxy forwards `Host`, and plugins pass `allow_other_host: true` where needed. |
 | `action_mailer.deliver_later_queue_name` | `nil` (default queue) | `:mailers` | Mail delivery jobs keep going to the queue the job workers of every release process (with a persistent queue adapter). | The workers of every supported release process the default queue. |
 | `action_view.preload_links_header` | `true` | `false` | A `Link` preload header per script and stylesheet: about 1-1.4KB more response headers on issue pages (measured), more with plugins. Next to the session cookie, that can exceed the proxy's header buffer (4KB by default with nginx), which then fails every page. Little benefit, the assets are in `<head>`. | Ops have checked the proxy buffers. |
 
